1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:07,680
Bolna open source. Can you run a voice AI
agent on your own servers, without the hosted dashboard?

2
00:00:07,810 --> 00:00:14,322
There is a public repository on GitHub,
under the MIT license, that holds the engine.

3
00:00:14,452 --> 00:00:19,202
You plug in your own provider keys, and run the orchestration yourself.

4
00:00:19,332 --> 00:00:25,631
This episode explains what the repo is, what
it is not, and when self hosting makes sense.

5
00:00:29,881 --> 00:00:45,972
The README calls Bolna an end-to-end, open source, production ready framework for building LLM
based voice conversations. The repository contains the entire orchestration platform, and it is MIT licensed.

6
00:00:46,102 --> 00:00:57,228
You describe an agent through JSON, and Bolna orchestrates the conversation. Pull
requests are welcome, and the project says it is actively looking for maintainers.

7
00:00:57,878 --> 00:01:02,030
The README lists three components, and only one of them is open.

8
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:08,978
The open repository is the orchestration platform. The
hosted APIs are built on top of it.

9
00:01:09,108 --> 00:01:14,539
The repo is MIT licensed. The hosted APIs are currently closed source.

10
00:01:14,669 --> 00:01:26,981
And the no-code playground, at platform dot bolna dot ai, uses those hosted APIs.
It is closed source too. So self hosting gives you the engine, not the dashboard.

11
00:01:27,631 --> 00:01:30,650
The README describes a clear development order.

12
00:01:30,780 --> 00:01:41,183
Any integration, enhancement, or feature first lands in this open source
package, because it forms the backbone of the hosted APIs and dashboard.

13
00:01:41,313 --> 00:01:46,244
After that, Bolna exposes new APIs, or changes existing ones.

14
00:01:46,374 --> 00:01:49,046
And third, it reaches the UI dashboard.

15
00:01:49,176 --> 00:01:52,034
So the repo is where the engine itself lives.

16
00:01:52,684 --> 00:02:03,802
Under the hood, the repo orchestrates voice conversations by
combining ASR, LLM, and TTS providers and models, over websockets.

17
00:02:03,932 --> 00:02:10,036
A telephony provider, like Twilio or Plivo, streams the call audio over a websocket.

18
00:02:10,166 --> 00:02:15,480
A transcriber turns speech into text. Deepgram is the documented example.

19
00:02:15,610 --> 00:02:22,860
An LLM agent writes the reply. The examples use OpenAI's gpt-4o-mini.

20
00:02:22,990 --> 00:02:30,834
And a synthesizer turns the reply back into audio,
ElevenLabs in the examples, to stream back over telephony.

21
00:02:30,964 --> 00:02:38,894
The README's own example calls it a
single coherent pipeline: transcriber, then LLM, then synthesizer.

22
00:02:39,544 --> 00:02:41,990
Here is the provider list from the README.

23
00:02:42,120 --> 00:02:47,417
Telephony: Twilio and Plivo. Exotel and Vonage are marked coming soon.

24
00:02:47,547 --> 00:02:56,636
Transcription: Deepgram and Azure, plus a self-hosted option,
a server speaking the OpenAI Realtime transcription protocol.

25
00:02:56,766 --> 00:03:09,565
LLMs go through the LiteLLM package, so OpenAI, DeepSeek, Llama,
Cohere, Mistral, and more. LLMs hosted with VLLM are supported too.

26
00:03:09,695 --> 00:03:23,433
And voices: AWS Polly, ElevenLabs, Deepgram, OpenAI, Azure, Cartesia, Smallest, Maya, and Kalpa.
For a deep dive on every provider, the README points to the hosted docs.

27
00:03:24,083 --> 00:03:28,927
Self hosting means you bring your own provider keys, in a .env file.

28
00:03:29,057 --> 00:03:35,031
Deepgram reads its auth token. ElevenLabs and OpenAI read their API keys.

29
00:03:35,161 --> 00:03:46,630
LiteLLM based models take an API key, and an API base URL
for a hosted LLM. Azure style models also take an API version.

30
00:03:46,760 --> 00:03:50,123
A VLLM server needs its base URL.

31
00:03:50,253 --> 00:04:00,092
Twilio needs the account SID, the auth token, and your Twilio phone
number. Plivo has the same three: auth ID, auth token, phone number.

32
00:04:00,222 --> 00:04:15,063
And AWS Polly uses your system wide AWS credentials. Because every key is yours, provider usage
is billed by each provider, on your own accounts. Check their pricing pages before you scale.

33
00:04:15,713 --> 00:04:17,912
So when does self hosting make sense?

34
00:04:18,042 --> 00:04:26,031
When you want the orchestration code itself, to read, change,
and debug. The hosted UI is not part of the repo.

35
00:04:26,161 --> 00:04:29,641
When you want every provider on your own accounts and keys.

36
00:04:29,771 --> 00:04:35,020
When you need a provider that isn't there yet.
You can add it yourself, as we'll see next.

37
00:04:35,150 --> 00:04:54,533
Or when models run on your own infrastructure, like an LLM served with VLLM, or a self-hosted transcriber. If you'd rather
not run servers at all, the hosted platform fits better, and the README invites you to contact Bolna about managed hosted offerings.

38
00:04:55,183 --> 00:05:00,850
Extending is documented. To add a telephony provider, like Vonage or Telnyx:

39
00:05:00,980 --> 00:05:04,706
First, make sure the provider supports bi-directional streaming.

40
00:05:04,836 --> 00:05:15,752
Add an input handler, under input handlers, telephony providers, extending the
telephony class. It defines how the provider's event packets are ingested.

41
00:05:15,882 --> 00:05:24,442
Add an output handler. It converts audio from the synthesizer
to a supported format, and streams it over the provider's websocket.

42
00:05:24,572 --> 00:05:32,583
And write a dedicated server, like the Twilio API
server in local setup, to initiate calls over websockets.

43
00:05:33,233 --> 00:05:50,796
Voices follow the same idea. The synthesizer README says: four registrations, one synthesizer class, and
one smoke run. Websocket providers subclass the stream synthesizer, and HTTP-only providers subclass the base synthesizer.

44
00:05:50,926 --> 00:06:00,968
And if a provider's server speaks the OpenAI speech API, you
skip the class, and point the OpenAI synthesizer at that server's URL.

45
00:06:01,618 --> 00:06:16,579
One honest note before you build on it. The README says Bolna is actively looking for maintainers, and that
maintenance has fluctuated, from a time crunch, not a lack of interest. Today it is maintained for the community.

46
00:06:16,709 --> 00:06:27,204
It also says the local example setup will be moved to a different repository.
So pin the version you build on, and check the README before you upgrade.

47
00:06:27,854 --> 00:06:28,952
Quick recap.

48
00:06:29,082 --> 00:06:34,181
The open repo is the orchestration engine, under the MIT license.

49
00:06:34,311 --> 00:06:38,536
The hosted APIs and the no-code playground are closed source.

50
00:06:38,666 --> 00:06:45,113
Telephony, transcriber, LLM, and synthesizer are orchestrated over websockets.

51
00:06:45,243 --> 00:06:48,818
You bring your own provider keys, in a .env file.

52
00:06:48,948 --> 00:06:52,860
And you can add telephony and TTS providers yourself.

53
00:06:52,990 --> 00:06:57,839
Next: self-host it with Docker, and place calls through Twilio or Plivo.

54
00:06:57,969 --> 00:07:03,729
This masterclass is created by Omkar Ghate. See you in episode fifty five.
